Spring implements @Transactional using AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming). When you call a @Transactional method on a Spring bean, Spring's proxy intercepts the call, begins a transaction, runs your method, and then commits or rolls back depending on the outcome.

Default behaviour: commits on success, rolls back on RuntimeException (unchecked exception). Checked exceptions don't trigger rollback by default — you need `@Transactional(rollbackFor = Exception.class)` if you want that.

Common usage in service layer:
```java
@Service
public class OrderService {
    @Transactional
    public void createOrder(Order order) {
        orderRepository.save(order);
        inventoryService.reduceStock(order.getItemId());
        // If reduceStock throws RuntimeException, the save is rolled back
    }
}
```

Important caveats:
1. Self-invocation doesn't work. If method A calls method B in the same class, and B is @Transactional, the transaction won't start — Spring's proxy isn't involved in internal calls.
2. @Transactional on private methods doesn't work — proxy only intercepts public methods.
3. @Transactional must be on a Spring-managed bean — not a plain Java object.

Placing @Transactional: service layer is the right place, not the repository layer (Spring Data repositories are already transactional by default).

Transaction propagation defines what happens when a @Transactional method is called from within an existing transaction.

REQUIRED (default): if there's already an active transaction, the method joins it. If there's no transaction, it starts a new one. All operations in the outer and inner method share the same transaction — if the inner method causes a rollback, it rolls back everything, including what the outer method did.

REQUIRES_NEW: always starts a brand new transaction, suspending the existing one. The inner method has its own transaction that commits or rolls back independently of the outer one. When the inner method finishes, the outer transaction resumes.

A classic use case for REQUIRES_NEW: audit logging. You want to record that a user attempted an operation even if the operation itself fails. If the audit log uses REQUIRES_NEW, it commits independently — even if the main transaction rolls back, the audit record is saved.

```java
@Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
public void logAuditEvent(AuditEvent event) {
    auditRepository.save(event);
    // This commits independently of the calling transaction
}
```

Other propagation options exist: SUPPORTS, MANDATORY, NOT_SUPPORTED, NEVER, NESTED — but REQUIRED and REQUIRES_NEW are by far the most commonly asked about.
